# Test messages of CPython's email package, https://github.com/python/cpython at commit 7731da1fe749eafe0d97f80ab0021d6fd1b47c8d,
# copied byte for byte with curl on 2026-09-27, each from the source URL followed by its path. The build verifies each file against
# its SHA-256 and fails on a mismatch, a missing file or an unlisted one (project/VendoredFiles.scala).
#
# Every file was read before vendoring. The messages are the email package's own test mail: obfuscated or example domains
# (zzz.org, dom.ain, example.com, wooster.local), test phrases, digests of "hello" messages, and public announcements (msg_36, an
# IETF Internet-Draft announcement). msg_15 is a message anonymized at source (addresses xx@xx.dk, bodies replaced by "Some removed
# text"). msg_41 is a spam sample whose address is xxx@example.com and which has no content. The expected values of these messages
# are in CPython's test code, not in files; the tests here compare the module with mime4j (EmailMessageReferenceTest) instead.
# What is left in, and none of it identifies a private person: the email package author's own addresses in his own test messages
# (barry@python.org in msg_04, 06, 08, 09, 10, 12, 12a and 44; barry@digicool.com in msg_02, 07, 13 and 17); the joke recipient
# cravindogs@cravindogs.com (msg_07, 08, 10); the public list address freebsd-isp@freebsd.org and its owner address (msg_32, 33),
# whose persons are already example.com addresses; the relay hosts fepD.post.tele.dk (195.41.46.149) and mail.groupcare.dk in
# msg_15's Received line; and the Message-ID domain teconcept.de in msg_46, whose persons are example.net and example.com.
#
# Excluded, each real mail of private persons:
#   msg_16.txt: a real delivery-failure report quoting a person's message, with their name, address and text.
#   msg_25.txt: a real bounce carrying a person's address and a real mailing-list bulletin.
#   msg_43.txt: a real virus-filter bounce listing recipients' (partly masked) addresses.
